## Phase 0 — Bootstrap (always, even mid-conversation)

Read the ledger before anything else. It is the memory that outlives the
context window.

```bash
cat .copilot/test-ledger/progress.json 2>/dev/null || echo "no ledger yet"
cat .copilot/test-ledger/conventions.md 2>/dev/null
```

If it exists, resume from `progress.json` — do not re-derive the plan, do not
re-scan targets marked `done`. If it does not exist, create it (schema in
**Memory protocol** below) and continue.

## Phase 1 — Discover the stack, do not assume it

Assumptions here are the largest single source of broken generated tests. Read,
never guess:

```bash
cat package.json
ls vitest.config.* vite.config.* jest.config.* 2>/dev/null
cat tsconfig.json 2>/dev/null
ls src/**/setupTests.* src/**/test-setup.* vitest.setup.* jest.setup.* 2>/dev/null
```

Record in `conventions.md`: runner and exact version; React version; the
installed `@testing-library/react` and `user-event` majors (`userEvent.setup()`
is v14+, older code uses the direct API — match what is installed); test file
location convention taken from the majority of existing files; coverage
provider and thresholds; path aliases; whether MSW, `jest-axe` and
`@testing-library/jest-dom` are already present.

Match existing conventions even where you would have chosen differently. A test
file that looks foreign to the repo will not be maintained.

## Phase 2 — Inventory and baseline

List every test that already exists, with file and line. This is the only
sanctioned answer to "does a test for X exist?" — never answer it from memory.

```bash
mkdir -p .copilot/test-ledger
grep -rnoE "^[[:space:]]*(it|test|describe)(\.[a-zA-Z]+)?(\([^)]*\))?\(\s*['\"\`][^'\"\`]+" \
  src --include='*.test.*' --include='*.spec.*' \
  | tee .copilot/test-ledger/inventory.txt
```

The `^[[:space:]]*` anchor is deliberate: it skips `it(` inside comments and
string literals, which is where naive greps go wrong. For the target module,
also read its existing test file **in full** — the grep gives you titles, not
what they assert.

Find modules with no test at all:

```bash
find src -type f \( -name '*.tsx' -o -name '*.ts' \) \
  ! -name '*.test.*' ! -name '*.spec.*' ! -name '*.d.ts' ! -name '*.stories.*' \
| while read -r f; do
    base=$(basename "$f"); base="${base%.*}"
    grep -rqE "from ['\"][^'\"]*/${base}['\"]|from ['\"]\./${base}['\"]" \
      src --include='*.test.*' --include='*.spec.*' || echo "NO TEST: $f"
  done
```

Then establish the baseline — you must know which failures you inherited:

```bash
npx vitest run --coverage 2>&1 | tail -40
```

Record total tests, failures and coverage in the ledger. **Pre-existing failures
are reported to the user, not silently fixed and not silently ignored.**

Two findings always get raised immediately:

- any `.only` — it silently disables the rest of that file's suite
- any `.skip` / `.todo` — each is an uncovered behaviour in disguise

```bash
grep -rnE "\b(describe|it|test)\.(only|skip|todo)\(|\bfit\(|\bxit\(" src --include='*.test.*'
```

## Phase 3 — Plan the behaviour matrix, then prove it is new

Read the target source file completely. Derive cases from what the code actually
does, in this order:

1. **Contract** — every exported symbol, every prop including defaults and
   optionals, every branch of every conditional render.
2. **States** — loading, empty, populated, error, disabled, and each variant of
   any union-typed prop.
3. **Interactions** — every handler prop, every form control, keyboard as well
   as pointer paths.
4. **Edge and boundary** — empty arrays, zero, negative, very long strings,
   `null`/`undefined` on optionals, rapid repeated interaction.
5. **Async** — resolution, rejection, in-flight state, cleanup on unmount, and
   any race the code permits between two in-flight requests.
6. **Accessibility** — role and accessible name for anything interactive. The
   query you use is itself the assertion.

### The duplicate gate

Duplicates are rarely byte-identical; they are the same behaviour reworded. So
compare normalised behaviour tokens, not strings. Write this once per repo:

```bash
cat > .copilot/test-ledger/dupcheck.mjs <<'EOF'
// Existing titles on stdin, planned titles as argv. Exit 2 = duplicate found.
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
const STOP = new Set(['a','an','and','as','at','be','by','component','correctly','for','given','in','is','it','of','on','or','properly','should','successfully','test','that','the','then','this','to','when','with','without']);
const SYN = { renders:'render', rendered:'render', displays:'render', display:'render', shows:'render', clicks:'click', clicked:'click', presses:'click', calls:'call', called:'call', invokes:'call', fires:'call', triggers:'call', errors:'error', fails:'error', throws:'error', updates:'update', changes:'update', submits:'submit', validates:'validate', validation:'validate', props:'prop', buttons:'button', inputs:'input', fields:'input', items:'item', rows:'row', lists:'list' };
const tok = s => [...new Set(String(s).toLowerCase().replace(/%[sdifjop#%]/g,' ').replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g,' ').split(' ').filter(w => w.length > 1 && !STOP.has(w)).map(w => SYN[w] || w))].sort();
const jac = (a, b) => { const B = new Set(b); const i = a.filter(x => B.has(x)).length; const u = new Set([...a, ...b]).size; return u ? i / u : 0; };
const existing = readFileSync(0, 'utf8').split('\n').map(l => l.replace(/^.*?[`'"]/, '').trim()).filter(Boolean);
const planned = process.argv.slice(2);
let dup = 0;
for (const p of planned) for (const e of existing) { const s = jac(tok(p), tok(e)); if (s >= 0.8) { console.error(`DUPLICATE ${s.toFixed(2)}: "${p}"  <->  "${e}"`); dup++; } }
console.log(dup ? `${dup} duplicate(s) — do not write these.` : `${planned.length} planned case(s): no duplicates.`);
process.exit(dup ? 2 : 0);
EOF
```

Run it against the existing titles for the module you are about to test:

```bash
grep -rhoE "^[[:space:]]*(it|test)(\.[a-zA-Z]+)?(\([^)]*\))?\(\s*['\"\`][^'\"\`]+" \
  src/components --include='*.test.*' \
  | node .copilot/test-ledger/dupcheck.mjs \
      "renders the display name from props" \
      "falls back to initials when there is no avatar" \
      "passes the user id to onSelect when activated"
```

Exit 2 means a duplicate — for example, "should render the label correctly"
scores 1.00 against an existing "renders the label". Remove the case, or, if the
behaviour truly differs, sharpen the title so it names the difference and note
in the ledger why it is not a duplicate. Never raise the 0.8 threshold to get
past it. Re-run the same command against the finished file after writing, to
catch duplicates you introduced inside the new file.

## Phase 4 — Write the tests

Patterns are in **Testing patterns** below. The rules that decide whether a test
is worth having:

- **Assert on user-visible output**, not implementation. Query by role and
  accessible name; `getByTestId` is a last resort and needs a one-line comment
  saying why the accessible query was impossible.
- **One behaviour per test**, and the title states the behaviour: "shows a
  validation message when email is empty", not "test email logic".
- **Assert the specific value.** `toHaveBeenCalledWith({ id: 7, name: 'Ada' })`,
  never a bare `toHaveBeenCalled()`. Weak assertions are what mutants live on.
- **`await userEvent.setup()`** per test. Use `fireEvent` only for synthetic
  events a user cannot perform (`scroll`, `resize`, file-input `change`).
- **`findBy*` for async appearance**, `waitFor` only for non-DOM assertions.
  Never a bare `setTimeout`, never a fixed sleep.
- **Mock at the boundary** — the network (MSW), the clock, modules you do not
  own. Do not mock the component's own children unless they are genuinely
  external or prohibitively heavy, and say so in a comment.
- **Deterministic** — fake timers restored in `afterEach`, no shared mutable
  fixtures, no reliance on test order.

Then run only that file, and make it pass without touching production code:

```bash
npx vitest run src/components/UserCard.test.tsx
```

## Phase 5 — Coverage to 100%

```bash
npx vitest run --coverage
```

Read the gap, not the percentage. Three kinds of gap need three responses:

- **uncovered lines** — a path no test walks. Write the test that walks it.
- **uncovered functions** — usually a callback never invoked: an `onError`, a
  cleanup return, a memo comparator. Trigger it.
- **uncovered branches** — one side of a ternary, `&&`, default argument or
  `if`. Render the case that takes the other side.

Also check what the summary table cannot show you:

```bash
# Coverage bought with ignore comments rather than tests:
grep -rnE "(istanbul|c8|v8)\s+ignore" src
# Source files the run never instrumented — these only appear with coverage.all: true:
node -e "const c=require('./coverage/coverage-final.json');console.log(Object.keys(c).length+' files instrumented')"
```

If a line is genuinely unreachable, delete the dead code — that is the fix, not
an exclusion. If the user confirms it must stay, record it in
`.copilot/test-ledger/coverage-exclusions.json` with `file`, `justification`,
`owner` and `expires`. An exclusion is a dated promise, not a permanent hole.

## Phase 6 — Mutation hunt

Coverage says the line ran. Mutation says the assertion noticed. Run narrow
while iterating on one module, full before declaring done:

```bash
npx stryker run --mutate "src/components/UserCard.tsx"
npx stryker run
```

For each survivor, strengthen an existing assertion so the mutant dies. A new
test walking the same path with the same weak assertion does not kill it and
does add a duplicate. The recurring causes:

| Survivor | What it means |
| --- | --- |
| `NoCoverage` (any mutator) | no test reaches this line — highest priority |
| `BooleanLiteral` on a default prop | you only ever rendered with the default |
| `ConditionalExpression` → `false` | the else branch renders something unasserted |
| `StringLiteral` → `""` | you asserted an element exists, not its text |
| `EqualityOperator` `<` → `<=` | no boundary test; add the exact-boundary case |
| `ArrowFunction` → `() => undefined` | you asserted a callback ran, not what it returned or passed |
| `ObjectLiteral` → `{}` | you asserted a payload was sent, not its contents |
| `MethodExpression` (`filter` → identity) | you asserted the list rendered, not that it was filtered |
| `BlockStatement` emptied | the side effect inside is unasserted |

Some mutants are genuinely equivalent — a `<` to `<=` on a value that can never
sit on the boundary. Mark those, with a real reason:

```ts
// Stryker disable next-line OptionalChaining: props.items is required and
// non-null per ButtonGroupProps; the guard is defensive only.
const count = props.items?.length ?? 0;
```

Two equivalent mutants in one file usually means the code has an unreachable
path that should be deleted instead.

## Phase 7 — Checkpoint and hand off

After **every** file reaches green on all three gates, update `progress.json`
before moving on. This is what makes a 200-file job survivable. Append anything
learned to `conventions.md` — a mocking pattern that worked, a provider wrapper
the repo needs, a component needing a `ResizeObserver` polyfill.

Report briefly: files completed, tests added, coverage, mutation score, and any
gate you could not meet with the reason.

