## Evidence rules

A test asserting an invented API fails loudly, which is annoying. A test
asserting invented *behaviour* that happens to pass is far worse: it freezes a
guess into the suite and maintainers will defend it.

Before writing any of these, read the evidence: a prop name/type/default (the
props interface); an exported symbol (the `export` statement); a hook's return
shape (its `return`); a DOM role or accessible name (the JSX plus the ARIA
mapping); a `data-testid` (the literal string in the JSX); text content (the JSX
or the i18n resource); an import alias (`tsconfig.json` / `vite.config.ts`
`paths`); a test utility (the file exporting it); an npm script (`package.json`);
a library API such as `userEvent.setup` (the installed major version).

If the evidence does not exist, one of three things is true: you misremembered
the library API (check the installed version); the repo genuinely lacks the
helper (create it explicitly as its own file and say you did); or the intended
behaviour is ambiguous (ask, naming the line and the two readings you are
choosing between).

**Never:** `it.todo`, `it.skip`, `xit`, empty test bodies,
`expect(true).toBe(true)`, `toBeDefined()` as a sole assertion, `// TODO`
stand-ins, a `describe` with no `it`, assertions on internal state or component
names, large `toMatchSnapshot()` blobs as the primary assertion, `any` casts to
force a type through, or a test copied from another repo or from memory.

**Self-check before writing a file:** Can I point at the source line for every
prop, role and string I used? Would each assertion fail if I changed the
corresponding line of the source? Does each test name describe a behaviour a
user or caller could observe? Have I imported anything that does not exist on
disk? Is there any expected value I inferred rather than read? The second
question is the important one — an assertion that survives the source being
wrong is a mutant survivor you can save yourself a round trip on.

