## Configuration

`vitest.config.ts` — do not lower these thresholds and do not widen `exclude`
to make a run pass:

```ts
test: {
  environment: 'jsdom',
  globals: true,
  setupFiles: ['./src/test-setup.ts'],
  pool: 'forks',        // true isolation; threads leak module state more often
  restoreMocks: true,   // spies restored between tests
  clearMocks: true,     // call history cleared between tests
  unstubEnvs: true,
  unstubGlobals: true,
  testTimeout: 10_000,
  hookTimeout: 10_000,
  retry: 0,             // a failing test must never be papered over
  coverage: {
    provider: 'v8',
    reporter: ['text', 'json', 'html', 'lcov'],
    all: true,          // files with zero tests must still appear in the report
    include: ['src/**/*.{ts,tsx}'],
    exclude: [
      'src/**/*.d.ts',
      'src/**/*.{test,spec}.{ts,tsx}',
      'src/**/*.stories.{ts,tsx}',
      'src/test-utils/**',
      'src/mocks/**',
      'src/test-setup.ts',
      'src/main.tsx',
    ],
    thresholds: { statements: 100, branches: 100, functions: 100, lines: 100 },
  },
}
```

`all: true` is the one people forget: without it, a component with no test file
is simply absent from the report and the percentage looks healthy. The `exclude`
list above is the maximum defensible one — type declarations, stories, the tests
themselves, test infrastructure, and the entry point that only calls
`createRoot`. Excluding a component to reach 100% is the failure this whole
skill exists to prevent.

`stryker.conf.json`:

```json
{
  "packageManager": "npm",
  "testRunner": "vitest",
  "coverageAnalysis": "perTest",
  "mutate": [
    "src/**/*.{ts,tsx}",
    "!src/**/*.{test,spec}.{ts,tsx}",
    "!src/**/*.stories.{ts,tsx}",
    "!src/test-utils/**",
    "!src/mocks/**",
    "!src/main.tsx"
  ],
  "reporters": ["html", "json", "progress", "clear-text"],
  "thresholds": { "high": 100, "low": 95, "break": 100 },
  "timeoutMS": 20000,
  "concurrency": 4,
  "disableTypeChecks": "src/**/*.{ts,tsx}",
  "ignoreStatic": false,
  "incremental": true
}
```

`coverageAnalysis: "perTest"` is what makes a full run finish in minutes rather
than hours: Stryker runs only the tests that actually cover each mutant.
`ignoreStatic: false` keeps module-level constants and default prop objects in
scope — slower, but that is exactly where a wrong default silently ships. If
full runs become unworkable, set it to `true` and record that decision in
`conventions.md` so the next session knows the score is no longer full-strength.

CI runs the gates in order and fails on any of them — never `continue-on-error`.
Scope the mutation run to changed files on pull requests and run it unscoped
nightly on main:

```bash
CHANGED=$(git diff --name-only "origin/$BASE_REF"...HEAD \
  | grep -E '^src/.*\.tsx?$' | grep -vE '\.(test|spec)\.' | paste -sd, -)
[ -n "$CHANGED" ] && npx stryker run --mutate "$CHANGED"
```

Always upload `coverage/`, `reports/` and `.copilot/test-ledger/` as artifacts
so a red build is diagnosable without a re-run.

